  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nCurrently near zonal flow is across the Dakotas, with a large\nupper level low digging through northern California. At the\nsurface a low pressure sits on the Wyoming/South Dakota border,\nwith an area of rain showers ahead of the low in eastern South\nDakota and the eastern half of North Dakota. The dry slot is in\neastern Montana and moving into western North Dakota, eroding\nclouds from west to east. \n\nTonight the surface low will slide east through South Dakota,\nwhile a surface high from southwestern Canada slides into the\nNorthern Plains. Widespread fog is forecast across western,\nnorthern, and central North Dakota tonight as winds calm. The \nvery near low pressure will continue to wrap the more moist air \ninto the state overnight, despite the nearing high pressure. \nThere is a low chance of frost in the far northwest where the \nhigh pressure will be near, however model runs today have \nincreased temperatures closer to 40 degrees and try to hang on \nto more cloud cover through the night. That being said, there \ncould be very localized frost in the lower valleys again. \n\nThe rest of this week, and into the weekend there will be near\ndaily chances of rain. That upper level low currently in\nnorthern California is forecast to move east to the northern\nRockies, while creating southwest diffluent flow over the\nDakotas beginning Thursday evening. Between Thursday evening and\nSunday, QPF is around 0.60 in the far west and far southeast.\nFriday will also be breezy from the pressure gradient force\nbetween a low pressure and high pressure. Easterly winds are \nforecast to be 20-30 mph across the southeast half of the state.\nTemperatures the rest of this week, and through the weekend will\nremain steady in the upper 60s to mid 70s, and lows in the upper\n40s and lower 50s. After this upcoming weekend, weaker zonal\nflow looks to return with a thermal ridge mid-week, when\ntemperatures could return back near 80.",
